    books_lookup_none: "Nothing found by this name – try typing the author's name below.",
    books_lookup_try_author: "Not here? The author's name can be typed below, too.",
    nm_ch_morning_stretch_help: "A simple 5 minutes:\n• Arms up and a long stretch – 30 seconds\n• Neck and shoulder circles – a minute\n• A gentle forward bend, knees soft – a minute\n• Side stretches, each side – a minute\n• Cat-cow on hands and knees – a minute\nGently and without pain – breathing slowly.",
    nm_ch_slow_meal_help: "How:\n• One meal a day that lasts at least 20 minutes\n• Putting the fork down between bites and chewing well\n• A glass of water at the start\nThe body needs about 20 minutes to feel full – eating slowly lets it catch up.",
    nm_ch_kitchen_closed_help: "After 8 PM:\n• Water, soda water and herbal tea\n• No food, no snacks and no “just a taste”\nTip: brushing teeth after dinner is a great “the kitchen is closed” signal. A late dinner is fine – as long as it's over by 8.",
    nm_ch_no_soda_help: "What counts as a sugary drink:\n• Soft drinks and cola, iced tea, energy drinks\n• Juices and nectars – even 100% fruit\n• Sweetened flavored water and cordials\nFine: water, soda water, water with lemon or mint, tea and coffee without sugar.",
    nm_ch_fruit_dessert_help: "Ideas:\n• A sliced apple with cinnamon, or a baked apple\n• Frozen grapes or frozen banana slices\n• Strawberries, melon, watermelon, mango, berries\n• Dates or dried fruit – in a small amount\nThe craving for something sweet is fine – it just gets fruit instead of cake or candy.",
    nm_ch_screen_free_meal_help: "How:\n• One meal a day without phone, TV, computer or tablet\n• Sitting at the table, eating slowly, noticing the taste and the fullness\nTip: the phone in another room, or face down in a drawer, for the whole meal.",
    nm_ch_walk_after_meal_help: "How:\n• 10 minutes of easy walking after one meal a day – after lunch or dinner works best\n• Outside, in the hallway or around the house – all count\n• A phone call while walking counts too\nA short walk after eating helps digestion and keeps energy steady.",
    nm_ch_veg_each_meal_help: "Ideas:\n• Breakfast: cucumber, tomato or peppers next to the eggs or cheese\n• Lunch: a salad or cooked vegetables on the side\n• Evening: a quick chopped salad, roasted vegetables or a vegetable soup\nFresh, cooked, frozen or in a salad – all count.",
    nm_ch_water_first_help: "How:\n• A big glass of water (about 300–500 ml) right after getting up\n• Before coffee or breakfast\nTip: a glass by the bed, or a bottle on the counter from the evening – and it happens by itself.",
    nm_ch_cinnamon_coffee_help: "How:\n• Half a teaspoon of cinnamon in the coffee, or in the milk before frothing\n• No sugar and no sweet syrup\n• With milk? Regular or plant milk without added sugar\nAny coffee works – espresso, black, filter or a latte.",
    nm_ch_lose5_month_help: "Every day, three things:\n• Within the calorie budget (a deficit)\n• A workout of 20 minutes or more\n• No added sugar\nThe challenge counts days, not kilos – weight goes down at its own pace. Weighing once a week, at the same time of day, gives the clearest picture.",
    nm_ch_menu_21_help: "How the day is marked:\n• ✓ on at least 3 menu meals – or on all of them when the menu has fewer\n• A planned free meal counts as a meal\n• A swapped meal (🔄) counts too – it's still from the menu\nThe day marks itself once there are enough ✓.",
    nm_ch_no_snacking_14_help: "What's fine:\n• The menu meals and snacks, at their times\n• Drinks: water, soda water, tea, coffee\nWhat counts as extra: tasting while cooking, finishing the kids' plates, a handful of nuts or a cookie between meals.\nTip: hungry between meals? Water first and 10 minutes of waiting – the next meal is close.",
    nm_ch_home_food_14_help: "What counts:\n• Anything made at home – even a sandwich, eggs or a salad\n• Food from home taken to work or school\nWhat doesn't: deliveries, takeaway, restaurants, fast food and ready meals bought outside.\nA planned free meal is allowed.",
    nm_ch_no_fried_14_help: "What counts as fried:\n• Fries and chips, schnitzel, falafel, fried fish\n• Doughnuts, latkes and fried snacks\n• Anything cooked in deep oil\nInstead: the oven, the grill, an air fryer, or a pan with a spoon of oil.\nTip: a home-made oven schnitzel is a great swap.",
    nm_ch_sport_14_help: "What counts (20 minutes or more):\n• A brisk walk, running, cycling, swimming\n• Gym, pilates, yoga, dancing, a home workout\n• Stairs or active play with the kids – 20 minutes in a row\nA workout of 20+ minutes logged in Workout Tracking marks the day by itself; otherwise it's marked here.",
    nm_ch_deficit_14_help: "How it works:\n• The daily budget = the plan (1,300 or 1,500) + calories from workouts logged that day\n• Everything eaten counts: the menu, drinks and anything added in the Daily Meal Tracker\n• The day counts when the ring stays within the budget\nTip: a planned free meal is fine – the rest of the day just stays lighter.",
    nm_ch_no_carbs_7_help: "What counts as a carb:\n• Bread, pita, rolls, crackers and rice cakes\n• Pasta, rice, couscous, bulgur, quinoa and noodles\n• Potatoes, sweet potatoes and corn\n• Breakfast cereals, oats and granola\n• Pastries, cakes, cookies, sugar and sweets\n• Legumes in large amounts (chickpeas, lentils, beans) and fruit juice\nInstead of the carb – add extra vegetables and protein: eggs, chicken, fish, tuna, cottage cheese, tofu.\nThe list is a guide – what exactly to cut is a personal choice.",
    nm_ch_no_sugar_5_help: "What to avoid:\n• White and brown sugar, honey, syrups and date syrup\n• Sweets, chocolate, cakes, cookies and ice cream\n• Sweetened drinks and juices\n• Sweetened yogurts and breakfast cereals\nFine: fruit, plain dairy, coffee and tea without sugar.\nTip: check labels – sugar also hides in sauces and ketchup.",
    nm_ch_help_btn: "What exactly counts?",
